# Subagent Delegation - rationale and mechanics

<!-- Installed by `gingugu harness`. A plain re-run refreshes this file only while
     it is an unedited copy of a version we shipped; once you edit it, it is
     yours and is left alone (use --force to replace it, with a .bak). -->

The operative rules live in the managed Subagent Delegation block of
`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. This file holds the *why* and the enforcement mechanics,
kept out of that file so they do not cost tokens on every request. Read it
before changing the delegation policy or an agent definition.

## Why implementation is delegable

The split is not hard-vs-easy, it is undecided-vs-decided. Deciding what to
build is Opus work. Typing it out once decided is "bounded reasoning against a
clear spec", which is the Sonnet row. Treating all coding as Opus-tier
conflates the two and leaves most of the work on the main thread for no reason.

Code is a *better* delegation target than research, not a worse one. A research
minion returns a conclusion with its reasoning discarded and the main thread has
to trust it. A coding minion returns an artifact the main thread can run.

The tests-first gate exists because the win only exists when verifying is
cheaper than writing - if every line has to be read to be trusted, the context
was spent anyway and nothing was gained. Tests-first makes the spec executable,
makes verification free, and forces the design to actually be finished before
handing off.

Fencing: a coding minion needs `Write`, `Edit` and `Bash`, and an allowlist
denies whole tools, not individual bash commands, so the usual read-only fence
does not apply. A deny rule matching command text is a speed bump, not a wall.

Ranking/scoring/retrieval math stays on the main thread because the failure
mode is code that passes the tests and is wrong in a way the tests cannot
resolve; delegating does not improve the instrument, it only changes who wrote
the green checkmark. Schema migrations stay because a shipped one can never be
fixed in place.

## The same split applies to the main thread's own model

Opus is required for greenfield - discovery, architecture, an undefined
problem. Once the task list is defined and the discovery is done, a whole
session can run on Sonnet. Choose that at the START of a session.

A mid-session model switch is NOT free: prompt caches are scoped to the model,
so the first call after a switch re-writes the whole context at cache-write
rates. On real pull-request turns (150-500k tokens of context, a handful of
calls) switching Opus -> Sonnet for the turn cost about 50% MORE than staying
on Opus with a 1-hour cache. It only pays on long turns with many calls.

## Why Opus work is never handed to a cold minion

- A minion returns a *conclusion* with its reasoning discarded. That arrives
  carrying authority at exactly the moment it is hardest to audit, and taking
  agent output at face value is a known failure mode.
- The real cost is not correctness, it is that the main thread never builds the
  context. Every follow-up question about the hard thing then gets answered from
  a summary of work it did not do.
- Genuinely independent Opus-hard tasks are rare. The common shape is one hard
  thread plus a pile of grunt work - and the grunt work is exactly what Haiku and
  Sonnet are for.

The escape hatch is a fork, not an Opus minion. A fork (`/subtask`) inherits the
entire conversation - context, system prompt, model and the parent's prompt
cache - so it is a branch of the main thread rather than a cold agent working
from a briefing. Every objection above is about coldness and none apply to a
fork.

## Enforcement mechanics

- A pinned `model:` in an agent definition is a strong default, not a hard
  ceiling - a per-invocation model parameter overrides it.
- `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL=sonnet` (set by `gingugu harness` in the `env` of
  `~/.claude/settings.json`) is the default for subagents whose definition names
  no model - the built-ins `Explore`, `Plan`, `general-purpose`, which otherwise
  inherit the main thread's model. Definitions that pin `haiku` keep it.
- `permissions.deny: ["Agent(model:opus)"]` (set by `gingugu harness` in each
  repo) blocks any spawn that explicitly names Opus. It does not catch a spawn
  that reaches Opus by inheritance - the env var above does.
- Harder, not set: `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL_FORCE=1` pins every subagent
  regardless of frontmatter (exceptions: forks, `model: inherit` skills).

## Agent definitions - notes

- `disallowedTools: Agent` is belt and braces alongside the `tools` allowlist:
  a minion that spawns its own minions can block on them and return nothing.
- The body becomes the agent's system prompt. It does not inherit the default
  Claude Code system prompt, but it does get the full CLAUDE.md hierarchy - so
  repo rules already apply and do not need restating.
- The allowlist denies whole tools, not individual bash commands, so any agent
  granted `Bash` is not fenced by it.

## What delegation is actually optimizing

Not raw token count. The wins are (a) tiering down, putting grunt work on
Haiku/Sonnet instead of Opus, and (b) keeping the main thread's context clean,
since a minion returns a conclusion instead of dumping ten files into the
window. Parallelism buys wall-clock, not dollars - and subagents cold-start
their own prompt cache. Optimize for the right experience and the right answer;
the tokens even out.
